from services.segmentation import Segmenter

# Finer than the old 2 s analysis windows: reps need several samples each to be
# resolvable at all (see rep_engine's note on resolution). The ceiling bounds
# GPU time on long footage by widening windows rather than adding more.
WINDOW_SECONDS = REP_WINDOW_SECONDS
MAX_WINDOWS = 96

# Tried in order; the first that answers wins. Order is by NAMING QUALITY, not
# just recency, because which model answers is the largest single source of
# variation in the exercise names -- a chain that falls through is a chain that
# renames the workout.
#
# gemini-3.5-flash is last on measured evidence: on the three-exercise
# reference clip it named the BENCH PRESS chapter "Barbell Back Squat" in 3 of
# the 4 runs where it answered, while the models above it named all three
# chapters correctly every time they answered. Three of four, not three of
# three -- it got the clip right once after this ordering was chosen, so the
# demotion is a hedge against a model that is usually wrong here, not a verdict
# that it always is.
#
# It is kept rather than removed because depth is what survives an outage --
# one verification run had all four models return 503 and fall back to local
# naming -- and a wrong name beats no report at all.
GEMINI_MODELS = (
    "gemini-flash-latest",
    "gemini-3.7-flash",
    "gemini-3.6-flash",
    "gemini-3.5-flash",
)
GEMINI_TIMEOUT_S = 300
# Above this, the video goes via the Files API instead of inline.
#
# 18 MB was wrong and is a separate latent bug from the retry one above: inline
# data is base64 in the request body, which inflates it by ~37%, and Gemini's
# cap is 20 MB for the WHOLE request. 18 MB of video is 24.6 MB on the wire --
# over the cap before the keyframes and prompt are added. Measured: a 13.15 MB
# file is 17.54 MB base64, already close.
#
# 10 MB -> 13.7 MB on the wire, leaving real headroom for several keyframe
# JPEGs and the prompt. Larger files take the Files API route, which costs an
# upload round trip (~11 s measured for 13 MB) but has no such ceiling.
INLINE_MAX_BYTES = 10 * 1024 * 1024
UPLOAD_POLL_S = 2

# Per-request wall clock for one generate_content call, in milliseconds.
#
# The SDK has NO default: a connection that opens and then stalls blocks the
# calling thread forever. Observed exactly that -- a Stage D upgrade sat in
# gemini_state "running" for over five minutes with no error, holding a
# worker thread, while the model chain below it never got a turn because the
# first model never returned. The report was fine (the local one had already
# been delivered), so nothing surfaced the hang at all.
#
# 120 s is roughly twice the slowest successful call measured on this clip,
# so a real answer is never cut off, and a hang now raises a timeout that
# _is_retryable() treats as the server's fault and hands to the next model.
GEMINI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 120_000

# How many times to walk the WHOLE model chain, and the wall clock past which
# no new attempt is started.
#
# One pass was not enough, measured. On a flaky afternoon the same 13 MB clip
# got 503 from one model, RemoteProtocolError from another and a clean answer
# from a third -- and which model drew which changed between runs seconds
# apart. A single pass therefore fails ENTIRELY on the unlucky ordering, which
# is exactly what a user saw: "Server disconnected", four models tried, local
# fallback, while the API was demonstrably answering.
#
# The deadline matters as much as the count: 3 passes x 4 models x a 120 s
# request timeout is 24 minutes of a worker thread in the worst case, for a
# report the user already has. 240 s stops new attempts long before that.
STAGE_D_MAX_PASSES = 3
STAGE_D_DEADLINE_S = 240
STAGE_D_BACKOFF_S = (3, 9)

SYSTEM_PROMPT = """You are a world-class sports kinesiologist and strength \
coach. You analyse athletic movement in any discipline -- gym lifts, basketball, \
tennis, golf, swimming, athletics, martial arts.

You receive four kinds of evidence. They are not equally trustworthy, and using \
them correctly is most of the job:

1. THE VIDEO. Your primary and only source of TECHNIQUE. Joint angles, posture, \
bar path, balance, sequencing -- all of it comes from watching.

2. KEYFRAME IMAGES at the moments a local model flagged. These are the exact \
frames the bounding boxes refer to, so use them to decide which box to cite.

3. A LOCAL EXERCISE CLASSIFIER's reading. It knows only 27 gym movements and \
nothing else, so on any other activity its label is meaningless and you must \
ignore it. Even on gym footage it is roughly 44% accurate -- treat it as a weak \
hint, never as the answer. You identify the activity yourself, from the video.

4. LATENT TRAJECTORY MEASUREMENTS. A frozen V-JEPA 2.1 encoder embeds each time \
window; a trajectory model then measures how far each window departs from the \
movement's own recent trend. High "kinetic error" means the motion pattern \
broke from what preceded it. This is real measurement, but it is UNITLESS and \
relative to this clip only -- never convert it to m/s, degrees or newtons.

   Critically: a kinetic-error spike says SOMETHING CHANGED, not that something \
is WRONG. Camera cuts, the athlete re-racking, a title card, someone walking \
past -- all spike it. It tells you where to look. The video tells you whether \
there is a fault there. If you look and the movement is fine, say it is fine \
and mark the phase optimal.

RULES

- Name phases in the vocabulary of the sport you actually see: a golf swing has \
a takeaway, transition and release; a squat has a descent, sticking point and \
lockout. Never "beginning / middle / end".
- session_type is "gym_reps" for countable repetitions of a resistance or \
bodyweight movement, "sports_drill" for continuous or open skill work.
- total_reps: use the supplied rep count when its confidence is high or medium. \
When it is low or none, count from the video yourself and prefer your own count.
- visual_proof is ONE object describing the SINGLE worst moment, which has \
already been located for you and is the timestamp of the keyframe image you \
were given. Pick the SAM candidate box that encloses the joint or equipment at \
fault and copy its normalized_bbox EXACTLY. Only if no candidate fits should you \
supply your own [ymin, xmin, ymax, xmax] in 0-1 normalised coordinates.
- target_object names what the box actually contains -- "left knee", "lumbar \
spine", "barbell" -- not a whole-body label like "athlete".
- If you look at that moment and the movement is genuinely sound, say so in \
issue_description rather than inventing a fault to fill the field. A flagged \
moment is where the motion CHANGED, and change is not proof of error.
- Ground every observation in something visible. If the camera angle hides the \
hips or the athlete leaves frame, say so instead of inventing detail. Confident \
fabrication is the worst failure here, and quoting a kinetic-error number does \
not make an invented fault real.
- form_score is YOUR coaching judgement, 0-100. Nothing in the pipeline \
measures it.
- actionable_cue is exactly ONE directive the athlete applies on their next \
repetition. "Drive your knees out over your little toes" -- not "work on form", \
and not a list of drills.
- If the footage shows no identifiable physical activity, set activity_detected \
to "unclear", score low, and explain why."""

# SAM runs per chapter, and each keyframe costs ~2.5 s plus a Gemini image.
# Four covers a normal session's worth of distinct lifts; past that the report
# stops being read and the request stops being fast.
MAX_PROOF_CHAPTERS = 4

# One Gemini call describes every chapter. N calls would multiply the slowest
# stage by the number of exercises, and the model needs the whole session in
# view anyway to tell a warm-up set from a working one.
